        <h2>Summary / Description</h2>
        <div class="description">
          <p><b>OBJECTIVES:</b> <br />
The aim of this 30-day bed rest study was to investigate the effects of short-term, high-intensity isotonic and isokinetic exercise training on working capacity, muscular strength and on orthostatic tolerance, posture and gait. Other data on muscle atrophy, bone mineralization and density, endocrine analyses concerning vasoactivity, fluid and electrolyte balance, muscle intermediary metabolism, performance and mood of the subjects were also acquired and are enumerated in summary ARCbedrest063. This report summarizes the effort to determine the effect of each exercise on the risk of developing kidney stones.</p>
<p><b>APPROACH:</b> <br />
The protocol consisted of a 7-day ambulatory control period, 30 days of -6<sup>o</sup> head-down bed rest and a 4.5-day ambulatory recovery period. Nineteen 32-42 year old male volunteers were divided into three groups: no-exercise controls (NE, n=5), isotonic exercise with an electronic Quinton ergometer, (IE, n=7), and isokinetic exercise on the Lido ergometer (KE, n=7). The exercise regimens were conducted near peak levels for 30 minutes in the morning and 30 minutes in the afternoon 5 days/week. Diets were controlled to provide 2,800 kcal/day for the NE group and 3,100 kcal/day for the two exercise groups. Twenty-four hour urine specimens were collected during the ambulatory pre-bed rest phase of the study and on the 8th and 28th days of bed rest. The specimens were sent to Mission Pharmacal Co. in Dallas, Texas, for analysis.</p>
<p><b>RESULTS:</b> <br />
Average caloric consumption of 2,678 kcal/d in NE, 2,833 kcal/d in IE and 2,890 kcal in KE resulted in mean losses of 1.01, 0.85, and 0.0 kg during bed rest in each group, respectively. Plasma volume was maintained only in the IE group and significantly reduced in NE and KE groups.</p>
<p>There were significant increases in urinary calcium in only 5 bed rest subjects who represented 1-2 from each exercise group. Urinary calcium increased 20% in control, 16% in IE and 2% in KE. The increases in urine calcium were not statistically significant probably because of small group size. However, the activity product of the stable form of calcium phosphate in urine increased in both exercising groups, especially the cyclists (IE). Brushite increased non-significantly from 2.1 to 4.5 in control and significantly from 2.7 to 6.4 in the cyclists and from 3.2 to 6.2 in the isokinetic group. These increases would favor stone formation and can be related to lower urine volumes or higher urinary pH (6.1 to 7.2 in control, 6.2 to 6.6 in IE and 6.5 to 7.2 in KE). Of interest is that differences in brushite present on the 8th bed rest day persisted to the 28th bed rest day in both exercise groups (significant only in the IE group because of a large variance in the KE group).</p>
        </div>

